---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import SectionHeading from '../components/SectionHeading.astro';
import { getProfile } from '../lib/content';
import { fullDate } from '../lib/format';

const profile = await getProfile();

// Update this date, and the test list below, whenever the site is re-tested. Never claim more than was tested.
const REVIEWED = '2026-11-16';
const reviewed = fullDate(`${REVIEWED}T12:00:00+13:00`);
---
<BaseLayout title="Accessibility" description="How carlos.nz aims to work for everyone, what has been tested, what is known to be missing, and how to report a barrier." welcome={false}>
  <section class="container-site pb-24 pt-12 md:pt-16">
    <SectionHeading
      level="h1"
      icon="material-symbols:accessibility-new"
      kicker="Accessibility"
      title="Accessibility statement"
      intro={`I want this site to work whatever device, settings or assistive technology you use. Last reviewed on ${reviewed}.`}
    />

    <div class="card mt-10 max-w-3xl p-6 md:p-8">
      <h2 class="text-h3 font-bold">The short version</h2>
      <ul class="mt-4 list-disc space-y-2 pl-5 text-muted marker:text-accent">
        <li>I aim to meet the <strong class="text-fg">Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG) 2.2, level AA</strong>.</li>
        <li>Automated checks run on every change. Screen reader testing is <strong class="text-fg">not done yet</strong>, so treat the site as a work in progress.</li>
        <li>If something does not work for you, tell me. I will fix it or give you the information another way.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="mt-4 flex flex-wrap gap-x-5 gap-y-1 font-display text-sm font-semibold">
        <a href="#screen-reader" class="inline-flex min-h-11 items-center text-brand underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-fg dark:text-accent">Using a screen reader?</a>
        <a href="#hard-to-read" class="inline-flex min-h-11 items-center text-brand underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-fg dark:text-accent">If the screen is hard to read</a>
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- For someone who already uses a screen reader: what to expect, in the order they will meet it. -->
    <section class="prose-post mt-12" aria-labelledby="screen-reader">
      <h2 id="screen-reader">Using a screen reader?</h2>
      <p>
        You do not need to change anything. The site is built for screen readers and checked automatically on every
        change, but I have <strong>not yet tested</strong> it with a screen reader myself. If something
        reads badly, <a href="#feedback">tell me</a>.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>The first visit.</strong> A welcome window opens once and takes focus on its title. It offers a few ways to adjust the site. Press Esc, or "Continue without changes", to leave the site as it is. It does not open again.</li>
        <li><strong>The first Tab stops</strong> on every page are "Skip to content", "Display settings" and "Accessibility statement".</li>
        <li><strong>Landmarks.</strong> Every page has a banner with the "Main" navigation (on a small screen it is behind the "Open menu" button and is called "Mobile"), one main region, and a footer with the "Footer" navigation.</li>
        <li><strong>Headings.</strong> Every page has one level 1 heading. Each section starts with a level 2 heading, and each card (a lab, a post, a repository) with a level 3 heading. Moving by headings gives you an outline of the page.</li>
        <li><strong>Cards</strong> start with their name. The status and the date come after it, as a sentence.</li>
        <li><strong>Links</strong> say where they go, and a link that opens a new tab says so.</li>
        <li><strong>Changes are announced:</strong> how many labs a filter shows, a copied link, and each problem in the contact form, next to its field and in a summary at the top.</li>
        <li><strong>Other languages.</strong> Words in te reo Māori and names in Portuguese are marked, so a screen reader with those voices pronounces them correctly.</li>
        <li><strong>"Listen to this page" is a separate voice</strong> that belongs to the site. It is there for people without a screen reader, and it never starts by itself. You will not need it.</li>
        <li><strong>Images</strong> have text descriptions, and decorative ones are hidden.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <!-- For someone who does not know these tools exist: what the site offers, then what their own device offers. -->
    <section class="prose-post" aria-labelledby="hard-to-read">
      <h2 id="hard-to-read">If the screen is hard to read</h2>
      <p>You do not have to struggle with small or pale text. On this site you can:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Have a page read aloud.</strong> Press "Listen to this page" on the home page, or "Listen to this post" on a post. To have the button on every page, open Display settings and choose "Show a Listen button on every page".</li>
        <li><strong>Make the text bigger and the colours stronger.</strong> Open Display settings, the button with the sliders icon at the top of every page, and choose a text size up to 200% and the "High contrast" theme.</li>
        <li><strong>Zoom the whole page.</strong> On a computer, hold Ctrl (Command on a Mac) and press the plus key. On a phone, spread two fingers on the screen. Nothing is cut off, up to 400%.</li>
        <li><strong>Read a shorter, simpler version</strong> of my story on the <a href="/about/easy-read/">Easy Read</a> page.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Your phone or computer can also help on every site, not only this one. It already
        has a <strong>screen reader</strong>: a tool that reads aloud what is on the screen and lets you use the device
        without seeing it. It is free and already installed. Look for "Accessibility" in the device's settings.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>iPhone, iPad and Mac:</strong> VoiceOver, and Zoom to magnify the screen.</li>
        <li><strong>Android:</strong> TalkBack, and Magnification.</li>
        <li><strong>Windows:</strong> Narrator, and Magnifier. <a href="https://www.nvaccess.org/">NVDA</a> is another free screen reader for Windows.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        In New Zealand, <a href="https://blindlowvision.org.nz/">Blind Low Vision NZ</a> helps people who are blind or
        losing their sight to learn these tools.
      </p>
    </section>

    <div class="prose-post">
      <h2 id="what-works">What you can do on this site</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Use the whole site with a keyboard. The first three Tab stops are "Skip to content", "Display settings" and this statement. While the Listen player is showing, a fourth stop, "Go to reading controls", takes you to it.</li>
        <li>Answer a short welcome window the first time you visit. It asks what would make the site easier for you (reading aloud, larger text and stronger contrast, simpler words, less movement, bigger buttons and links), shows each choice straight away, and saves nothing until you press "Use my choices". "Continue without changes" or Esc leaves the site as it was. It asks; it does not try to work out who you are.</li>
        <li>Open <strong>Display settings</strong> from the header at any time to choose a light, dark or high-contrast theme, larger text, wider text spacing, a narrower line length, reduced motion, bigger buttons and links, or a Listen button on every page. They are saved only in your browser and never sent to me. The site also follows your device's own contrast and reduced motion settings.</li>
        <li>Read text that is never smaller than 14 pixels, and use buttons, menu items and links that are at least 44 pixels tall. "Bigger buttons and links" in Display settings makes them 48 pixels and larger.</li>
        <li>Zoom the page to 400% without losing content or scrolling sideways. On a short screen, such as a laptop at 400% zoom or a phone on its side, the header scrolls away with the page instead of covering it.</li>
        <li>Read a simpler version of my About section in <a href="/about/easy-read/">Easy Read</a>, and look up an abbreviation in the <a href="/glossary/">glossary</a>. Each page also ends with "Words on this page", a plain list of the abbreviations and technical terms it uses, with their meanings.</li>
        <li>Have any page or post read aloud with the Listen button, which uses your browser's own voices. You can pause, resume, stop and change the speed, and the part being read is outlined. It starts only when you press a button, and the site never turns the microphone on.</li>
        <li>See where you are in a post from the breadcrumb at the top, and which page you are on in the main menu.</li>
        <li>Read post images through their text descriptions. Alt text is checked before a post is published.</li>
        <li>Send a message through a form that explains each problem next to the field and in a summary at the top.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="testing">How it has been tested</h2>
      <p>On {reviewed}:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Automated checks with axe-core on every page, in light, dark and Windows high-contrast (forced colours) modes.</li>
        <li>Automated keyboard checks: the skip link, the mobile menu and the contact form errors.</li>
        <li>A check that the page reflows at a 320 pixel width, and that animation stops when reduced motion is requested.</li>
        <li>A check of the colour contrast of text, form field borders and focus rings in the light, dark and high-contrast themes.</li>
        <li>Automated checks of the Display settings: they open and close with the keyboard, are remembered, and the pages still pass axe and reflow at 200% text size with wide spacing.</li>
        <li>Automated checks of the welcome window: it opens once, names itself, takes focus, can be used with the keyboard alone, previews and then keeps or undoes each choice, passes axe in light, dark and high-contrast themes, and fits a 320 pixel screen at 200% text.</li>
        <li>Automated checks that reading aloud starts only on a button press and can be paused, resumed and stopped, and that "Bigger buttons and links" gives controls a target of at least 48 pixels.</li>
        <li>Automated checks that "Words on this page" lists only words that really appear on the page, is plain text with one link, is read aloud with the page, and that every abbreviation on the main pages is in the glossary.</li>
        <li>Automated checks that no visible text is smaller than 14 pixels, that every control standing on its own is at least 44 pixels tall and wide, and that nothing moves on its own for more than five seconds.</li>
        <li>Automated checks on two phone screens (iPhone SE and Pixel 7, with touch, at the normal text size and at 200%): the menu opens, says whether it is open and every link can be reached; nothing is cut off or too small to tap; the read-aloud player never covers the focused element and scrolls with the page on a phone turned sideways; form fields are large enough that iPhone Safari does not zoom in; the page behind an open window stays still.</li>
        <li>Automated checks of what a screen reader receives: each card's heading comes before its status and date, no heading repeats "(opens in a new tab)", links make sense on their own, statuses read as sentences, durations are in whole words, the welcome window tells screen reader users first that they need not change anything, "Copy link" and the Labs filter announce their result, and contact form fields announce their limit, and "Error" only when there is one.</li>
        <li>Automated checks that the home page says the site is built for screen readers, the keyboard and zoom, that its "Listen to this page" button reads only after a press and is not shown when the browser cannot read aloud, and that this statement has its screen reader guide and its guide for people who find the screen hard to read.</li>
        <li>Automated checks that every Tab stop on the home page, Labs, Posts and Contact shows where the keyboard focus is, that every page fits a 320 pixel screen, at the normal text size and with 200% text, wide spacing and the standard text spacing overrides, without scrolling sideways or cutting anything off at the edge, that buttons keep their label on one line on a desktop, that the header does not cover a short screen, and that on a post the text comes before the sidebar in the page and on screen.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Automated tools find only part of the barriers that exist. I have <strong>not yet</strong> tested with
        screen readers such as NVDA or VoiceOver, or with disabled users. When I do, I will list it here.
      </p>

      <h2 id="known-issues">Known issues</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>The contact form needs JavaScript and the Cloudflare Turnstile spam check. If you cannot use it, contact me on LinkedIn instead.</li>
        <li>Posts published before {reviewed} may have images without good descriptions. Tell me which one and I will fix it.</li>
        <li>The Easy Read page has not been checked by people who use Easy Read. Tell me if it is unclear.</li>
        <li>Reading aloud depends on the voices your device has, so the quality varies. It always starts at the top of the page, code examples are skipped, and the outline follows a paragraph at a time, not a word at a time. A browser with no text-to-speech does not show the option at all.</li>
        <li>"Words on this page" is built by a script, so without JavaScript it is missing. The <a href="/glossary/">glossary</a> page works without it. A term shows only if it is in the glossary, and the list is matched by spelling, so a word used in an unusual form may be missed.</li>
        <li>Choosing "Simpler words" opens the Easy Read version of my About section. The rest of the site is not rewritten in Easy Read.</li>
        <li>"Stronger contrast" uses the dark high-contrast theme. There is no light high-contrast theme yet.</li>
        <li>The welcome window has not been tested with screen readers or with disabled users, only with automated checks and the keyboard.</li>
        <li>I do not aim to meet every level AAA criterion, such as sign language for video or a reading level for all text.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="feedback">Report a barrier</h2>
      <p>
        Use the <a href="/contact/?topic=other">contact form</a> and choose "Something else", or
        {profile.linkedin_url
          ? <>message me on <a href={profile.linkedin_url} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer me">LinkedIn<span class="sr-only"> (opens in a new tab)</span></a></>
          : 'reach me through the contact page'}.
        Say which page and what you were trying to do. I read every message, and I treat a barrier as a bug, not a feature request.
      </p>
      <p>
        This statement was last reviewed on {reviewed}. The privacy side of the site is explained in the
        <a href="/privacy/">privacy notice</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
